{
  "requirement": "An evidence detail page MUST continue to show the current requirement or example and validation findings when evidence is missing or invalid. It MUST show readable placeholders for missing rationale or inputs and expose the contents of an unreadable JSON record.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement is met: the code is marked `{impl}`, and the tests cover the missing-evidence and broken-JSON cases. I did not run the tests. I judged from the source and the test assertions.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `render_detail_sections` (src/index.rs:657–760, marked `{impl}`) is the shared renderer. `render_detail_page` (463–494) wraps it for book pages, and `info_markdown` reaches it through `render_cli_detail_page`.\n  - It always prints the badge and the current definition text, as a \"Requirement\" or \"Example\" block quote, before looking at the evidence. Missing or invalid evidence therefore cannot hide the definition.\n  - It prints \"Validation findings\" whenever `errors` is non-empty. The text is HTML-escaped.\n  - It parses the record leniently with `serde_json::from_str(...).ok()` and `as_object`. A missing, malformed or non-object record never aborts the page.\n  - It shows \"No rationale recorded.\" when the rationale is absent, not a string, or blank.\n  - It shows \"No inputs recorded.\" when the inputs are absent, not an array, or empty.\n  - If the record text exists but does not parse into an object, it adds an \"Unreadable evidence record\" section. That section prints the raw contents in a `json` code block through `push_code_block`, which chooses a fence long enough to survive backticks in the content.\n  - Malformed individual inputs fall back to pretty-printed raw JSON inside `render_input`.\n- `LoadedDetailEvidence::load` (151–206, marked `{impl}`) tolerates missing files and read failures. A read failure becomes a finding of the form \"cannot read evidence: …\". It keeps the raw record text so the page can show it. It maps the outcome to a Missing, Invalid, DeterminationFalse, Valid or Fixme state, and that state feeds the page badge.\n- I found no behavior in the marked code that goes beyond this requirement or another requirement it also cites. The extra `{impl}` tags on these functions name other requirements the code also implements.\n\n**Tests**\n- `invalid_evidence_detail_remains_readable` (1442–1502) checks two cases.\n  - Broken JSON `{broken` with a finding. The page keeps the definition, shows \"Validation findings\", shows both placeholders, and shows the \"Unreadable evidence record\" section with the raw ```` ```json\\n{broken\\n``` ```` block.\n  - Missing evidence. The page keeps the definition and shows both placeholders.\n- `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` (1038–1211) goes through `preprocess_book` for real fixtures. A stale-text record produces an Invalid page containing \"requirement text changed\", and a missing record gives a Missing state with a page.\n- `index_lists_examples_and_links_their_detail_pages` (1504–1591) covers an example with no evidence. The page shows the example text, \"Validation findings\", \"missing for example\", and \"No rationale recorded.\"\n\n**Minor gaps, none disqualifying**\n- A non-UTF-8 file fails `read_to_string`, so its raw contents are not shown. The failure does appear as a finding.\n- A valid-JSON non-object record, such as `[]`, is not tested directly. The code path is the same as for broken JSON.\n- There is no CLI-specific test of the invalid-detail behavior. It uses the same renderer.\n- There are no FIXME markers or acknowledged gaps.",
